   478	## 7. The cuspidal horn: Lin–Zaidenberg, CUSP-KILL, and the `pi_1` cage
   479	
   480	Case **(A)** — every singular point of `A_F` unibranch — collapses to one curve
   481	up to a target automorphism, and then to a covering-space question.
   482	
   483	> **PROPOSITION 7.1.** In case (A), `A_F` is homeomorphic to `C`, hence by the
   484	> **Lin–Zaidenberg** theorem there is `α ∈ Aut(A^2)` with
   485	> `α(A_F) = {x^p = y^q}`, `gcd(p,q) = 1`; SMOOTH-KILL forces `p, q >= 2`.
   486	> Moreover `s = 1`, `a_p = 1`, `nu = 0`, `K_p = a - 1` (Prop. 6.1 and `(L)`).
   487	>
   488	> *Proof.* `eta : D~ ≅ A^1 -> D` (Lemma A) is a proper morphism, bijective
   489	> because every point of `D` — smooth or singular — is unibranch. A proper
   490	> continuous bijection is a homeomorphism, so `D` is a topologically embedded
   491	> line. Lin–Zaidenberg (Invent. Math. 68 (1982) 1–17: an irreducible plane
   492	> affine curve homeomorphic to `C` is `Aut(C^2)`-equivalent to `{x^p = y^q}`)
   493	> applies. `s = 1` because `{x^p=y^q}` with `p,q >= 2` has exactly one singular
   494	> point. ∎
   495	
   496	Write `G_{p,q} := pi_1(C^2 \ {x^p=y^q}) = <α, β | α^p = β^q>` (the curve is a
   497	cone over the `(p,q)` torus knot, so the complement deformation retracts to
   498	`S^3 \ K_{p,q}`); `Z := Z(G_{p,q}) = <z>`, `z = α^p = β^q`, and
   499	`G_{p,q}/Z ≅ Z/p * Z/q` with trivial centre. `F` is proper and étale over
   500	`A^2 \ D`, so
   501	
   502	```text
   503	(7.1)  F : C^2 \ E  -->  C^2 \ D ,  E := F^{-1}(D),  finite etale of degree N,
   504	       H := pi_1(C^2 \ E)  <=  G_{p,q}  of index N.
   505	```
   506	
   507	`D_0 = D \ {p} ≅ C^*`, so `E_0 := F^{-1}(D_0) -> C^*` is an `a`-sheeted
   508	covering; let `j` be its number of connected components, so `j <= a`, and `E`
   509	has exactly `j` irreducible components (`a_p = 1` puts a single, unibranch point
   510	of `E` over `p`, so at most one component of `E_0` acquires it).
   511	
   512	> **THEOREM CUSP-KILL.** In case (A), if `E` is irreducible (equivalently
   513	> `j = 1`; in particular whenever `a = 1`) then `F` is an automorphism —
   514	> contradiction. Hence `j >= 2` and `a >= 2`.
   515	>
   516	> *Proof.* `j = 1`: `E_0` is a connected `a`-sheeted cover of `C^*`, so
   517	> `E_0 ≅ C^*`; `E_0` is smooth (`F` étale over the smooth `D_0`); `E = E_0 ∪
   518	> {y_0}` with `(E,y_0) ≅ (D,p)` analytically (`F` a local biholomorphism), hence
   519	> unibranch and singular. So `E~ = C^* ∪ {1 place} ≅ A^1` (affine, so not
   520	> `P^1`), the normalization of `E` is bijective, and `E` is homeomorphic to `C`.
   521	> Lin–Zaidenberg again: `E ≅ {x^{p'} = y^{q'}}`, and the analytic type of its
   522	> unique singular point identifies `{p',q'} = {p,q}`. Hence `H ≅ G_{p,q}`.
   523	>
   524	> Put `Z_H := Z ∩ H`, `Delta := H/Z_H ≅ HZ/Z <= G/Z = Z/p * Z/q` of index `M`,
   525	> and `N = M·[Z : Z_H]`. `Z/p * Z/q` is virtually free, infinite and
   526	> nonelementary (`p,q >= 2`, `gcd(p,q) = 1` excludes `Z/2 * Z/2`), so the
   527	> centralizer of its finite-index subgroup `Delta` is trivial; hence
   528	> `Z(H) = Z_H` and `Delta ≅ H/Z(H) ≅ Z/p * Z/q`. Rational Euler characteristic
   529	> is multiplicative in finite index: `chi(Delta) = M·(1/p + 1/q - 1)` and also
   530	> `= 1/p + 1/q - 1 ≠ 0`, so `M = 1`, i.e. `HZ = G`. `Z` central then makes
   531	> `H ◁ G` with `G/H ≅ Z/Z_H` cyclic of order `N`: the covering (7.1) is Galois,
   532	> so `C(x,y)/C(F_1,F_2)` is a Galois extension. By **Campbell** (Math. Ann. 205
   533	> (1973) 243–248) a Keller map with Galois field extension is invertible. ∎
   534	
   535	For `j >= 2` the same computation is a cage rather than a kill. `H^{ab} =
   536	H_1(C^2 \ E) = Z^j` is torsion-free, and `Delta^{ab}` is `H^{ab}` modulo the
   537	image of the cyclic `Z_H`, so `Delta ≅ F_r * (Z/p)^{*u} * (Z/q)^{*v}` (Kurosh)
   538	has `r ∈ {j-1, j}` and cyclic torsion, forcing `u <= 1`, `v <= 1`; and
   539	`chi(Delta) = 1 - r + u(1/p - 1) + v(1/q - 1) = M(1/p + 1/q - 1)`.
   540	
   541	> **THEOREM CUSP-CAGE.** In case (A) with `j >= 2`, writing `t := pq - p - q`:
   542	> `(u,v) = (1,1) ⟹ r = (M-1)t/(pq)`, and `gcd(pq, t) = 1`, so `pq | M - 1` and
   543	> **`N >= M >= pq + 1`**; `(u,v) = (1,0) ⟹ p | M-1` and `q | M`;
   544	> `(0,1) ⟹ q | M-1`, `p | M`; `(0,0) ⟹ pq | M`. In every case `M >= 2`, so
   545	> `HZ ≠ G` and `N` has a divisor `M >= 2` of the listed congruence type.
   546	> Equivalently, in the global Milnor fibration
   547	> `h ∘ F : C^2 \ E -> C^*` (pullback of the quasi-homogeneous fibration of
   548	> `x^p - y^q`), the monodromy on `H_1` of the generic fibre has eigenvalue `1`
   549	> with multiplicity `j - 1 >= 1`.
   550	
   551	One consequence of the last step of CUSP-KILL is free of case (A) and worth
   552	recording separately, because the representation lanes can use it directly:
   553	
   554	> **COROLLARY 7.2 (non-normality, every profile).** For any noninvertible plane
   555	> Keller map, `H := pi_1(C^2 \ F^{-1}(A_F))` is a **non-normal** subgroup of
   556	> index `N` in `pi_1(C^2 \ A_F)`. *Proof.* `H ◁ G` makes the finite étale
   557	> covering `C^2 \ F^{-1}(A_F) -> C^2 \ A_F` Galois, hence
   558	> `C(x,y)/C(F_1,F_2)` Galois, hence `F` invertible by Campbell. ∎
   559	>
   560	> Equivalently: the monodromy `rho : pi_1(C^2 \ A_F) -> S_N` of a Keller
   561	> counterexample is transitive but **not** regular — its image has order `> N`.
   562	> This is a gate on every meridian-cycle-type search the campaign runs, and it
   563	> is cheap to check.
   564	
   565	**Honest limits.** The cage does not empty case (A). Worked check at `N = 4`
   566	(§10 control 6): `a = 1` dies by CUSP-KILL (`j = 1`); `a = 2` needs the meridian
   567	`m = α^s β^t` (`sq + tp = 1`) to map to a transposition under a transitive
   568	`rho : G_{p,q} -> S_4`, which happens, and `(p,q) = (3,4)`, `M = 4`,
   569	`(u,v) = (1,0)`, `r = j = 2` satisfies every congruence above. So case (A) at
   570	`N = 4` is **not** closed here: `OPEN[MPRIME-CUSP-J2]`, §9.
